  "title": "What is a prophet?",
  "aliases": [
    "a prophet question",
    "a prophet in the bible",
    "a prophet overview"
  ],
  "summary": "A prophet is someone called to deliver a message from God. The message may confront injustice, warn of consequences, encourage a weary people, or promise restoration; predicting the future is only one part of the role. Deuteronomy 18 asks how to test a prophetic word, while the stories of Samuel, Isaiah, Jeremiah, and Ezekiel show prophets being commissioned to speak in particular situations.",
  "historical_context": "The cited texts place prophets in different settings. Samuel speaks in the early period of Israel’s leadership; Isaiah receives a commission in a vision; Jeremiah is appointed to speak to nations and kings; Ezekiel is sent to a resistant community in exile; and Amos challenges public worship separated from justice. These examples show prophets addressing real communities and choices, not speaking in one uniform role.",
  "ancient_near_east_context": "The biblical passages themselves show what prophetic work involves: Samuel receives and delivers a message, Isaiah sees a vision and is sent, Jeremiah is appointed to speak, and Ezekiel is told to address a rebellious people. Those examples explain the role without relying on an unsupported comparison to other courts or advisers.",
