  "literary_context": "Prophets speak through different forms. Deuteronomy 18 discusses how a prophetic claim is tested; 1 Samuel 3 tells a call story; Isaiah 6 and Jeremiah 1 describe commissioning; Amos 5 delivers a warning about worship and justice; and Luke 4 shows Jesus reading Isaiah in a new setting. Pay attention to the form and audience of each passage before deciding what the message is doing.",
  "covenantal_significance": "Many prophetic messages call people to live faithfully toward God and one another. Amos insists that worship cannot excuse injustice; Jeremiah and Ezekiel are commissioned to speak into crisis; and Luke 4 presents Jesus applying Isaiah’s message to his ministry. Prophetic words can announce judgment or hope, but their force depends on the situation and the response they call for.",
  "authorship_positions": [],
  "date_ranges": [],
  "original_audience": "",
  "historical_setting": "",
  "canonical_role": "The Bible’s prophetic writings connect present crises with future hope, but they do so through specific messages and later readings. The Gospels and Acts interpret some prophetic texts in relation to Jesus; Luke 4 and Acts 3 show examples. Those later connections belong alongside, rather than in place of, each prophet’s original audience and message.",
